🧩 𝗣𝗢𝗦𝗧 𝟭: 𝗠𝗼𝗻𝗼𝗹𝗶𝘁𝗵𝗶𝗰 𝘃𝘀 𝗠𝗶𝗰𝗿𝗼𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀 𝗔𝗿𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗲𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲

💭 𝗛𝗼𝗼𝗸: Every engineering team eventually asks this question: "Should we split this into microservices?" Here's how to actually answer it. 👇

🏛️ 𝗠𝗼𝗻𝗼𝗹𝗶𝘁𝗵𝗶𝗰 𝗔𝗿𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗲𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲

 A single codebase and a single deployable unit. Your auth, orders, payments, and inventory modules 𝗮𝗹𝗹 𝗹𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝘁𝗼𝗴𝗲𝘁𝗵𝗲𝗿, share one database, and get deployed as one artifact.


┌─────────────────────────────┐

│      MONOLITH APPLICATION    │

│  ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐       │

│  │  Auth  │ │ Orders │  ...  │

│  └────────┘ └────────┘        │

│         one shared DB        │

└─────────────────────────────┘



🧬 𝗠𝗶𝗰𝗿𝗼𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀 𝗔𝗿𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗲𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲



 The same functionality, split into 𝗶𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗽𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗹𝘆 𝗱𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀, each with its own database, communicating over APIs or message queues.



 Client → API Gateway

              │

   ┌──────────┼───────────┐

┌──────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌───────────┐

│ Auth  │   │ Orders│  │ Inventory│

│  DB    │   │  DB │   │ DB       │

└──────┘  └────────┘  └───────────┘



🍔 𝗣𝗿𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗮𝗹 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲

 A food delivery startup with 𝟱,𝟬𝟬𝟬 𝘂𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘀 runs fine as a monolith — one team, one deploy, fast iteration. 🏃‍♂️

At 𝟱 𝗺𝗶𝗹𝗹𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝘂𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘀, the "Order" service gets hammered during lunch hours while "Reviews" sits idle. 📈

⚠️ In a monolith, you scale the 𝘄𝗵𝗼𝗹𝗲 𝗮𝗽𝗽 just to handle Orders.

 ✅ In microservices, you scale 𝗼𝗻𝗹𝘆 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗢𝗿𝗱𝗲𝗿 𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 — cheaper, faster, and a bug in Reviews can't take down Checkout.


⚖️ 𝗪𝗵𝗲𝗻 𝘁𝗼 𝗨𝘀𝗲 𝗪𝗵𝗶𝗰𝗵

🏛️ 𝗠𝗼𝗻𝗼𝗹𝗶𝘁𝗵

 → Small team, early-stage product

 → Simple domain, fast MVP needed

 → Limited ops/DevOps maturity

🧬 𝗠𝗶𝗰𝗿𝗼𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀

 → Large team, multiple squads

 → Complex domain, independent scaling needed

 → Strong CI/CD and observability in place

💡 𝗥𝘂𝗹𝗲 𝗼𝗳 𝗧𝗵𝘂𝗺𝗯

 Start monolithic. Split into microservices only when a 𝘀𝗽𝗲𝗰𝗶𝗳𝗶𝗰, 𝗽𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗳𝘂𝗹 scaling or team-coordination problem demands it — not because it's trendy. 🎯



